                "reviews": [
                    {
                        "recommendationid": "239509968",
                        "author": {
                            "steamid": "76561198009049000",
                            "num_reviews": 15,
                            "playtime_forever": 1999,
                            "playtime_last_two_weeks": 0,
                            "playtime_at_review": 1999,
                            "last_played": 1746309518
                        },
                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "4/10 - \"Industries of Titan\" is city-build with wonderful aesthetics, and the unique idea of designing the insides of your factories and spaceships. The whole game falls apart later on, as there is basically no end-game, highly simplified and unsatisfying combat, and a sudden end of the \"build the insides of your factories\" with \"pre-made\" buildings during the mid-game.\n\n[h1] Pros [/h1]\n[list]\n    [*]One of the most aesthetically pleasing city-builders that I have seen in a long time. The designs of the buildings, roads, ships and environments fit so well together with it's very dystopian theme.\n    [*]Being able to build the inside of your buildings and ships to influence what they do specifically and to what degree is awesome. This mini-game is the main draw of the game and appeal. I just wish there would have been a couple more options.\n    [*]The dynamic gas system for both the fuel gas and pollution is pretty neat, even though I have seen it being implemented in a similar way in other games. These holes create harvestable gas that spreads out in all directions. If you place buildings on one side, the gas will stop spreading in that direction and be harvested instead. This can create issues though, as for example shipyards next gas around it to fuel their ships, otherwise they will not be able to start. There is sadly no way to harvest gas and send it into the shipyard.\n[/list]\n[h1] Cons [/h1]\n[list]\n    [*]There is nothing to do in mid-game and late-game. After about 10h - 15h in, I was disappointed in realising, that that is all the game had to offer.\n    [*]It is a far more simple game than I anticipated at first. There is a total of 3 resources in the game, no complex production chains to manage, and the citizens have basically not requirements or needs.\n    [*]A bit later in the game, they \"replace\" the empty factories, that you can fill yourself with modules to give them functions and purpose, with buildings that are \"pre-made\". They server a specific purpose and their insides cannot be changed. One of the most interesting and integral part of them is removed with these building. It makes the game feel way less involved any drastically more simple.\n    [*]The patches of water cannot be interacted with or used. It just acts as nothing more than an obstacle and feels wasted.\n    [*]The combat is barebones and uninteresting. There is no enemy type variety, the laser as the only weapon type and the combat is limited to ships doing 1v1s. There is no selecting of multiple ships and you have to give commands to each of your ships separately, one at a time.\n    [*]You cannot send gas needed to fuel the ships directly into the shipyard. You need to place the shipyard in a pocket of gas, otherwise they will not be able to launch.\n[/list]",
